 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Gate drive",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Threshold lies between -1.3 V and -2.3 V at -250 µA. On-resistance is specified at two drive levels: 11.5 mΩ max at -10 V and -14 A, 18.5 mΩ max at -4.5 V and -10 A. The gate tolerates ±25 V and leaks at most ±100 nA there. Charging the gate to -10 V takes 40 nC typical (60 nC max), to -4.5 V 20 nC typical (30 nC max); internal gate resistance is 3.2 Ω typical, 6.4 Ω max."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heat",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Thermal figures apply on a 1 in² pad of 2 oz copper on FR-4 in still air at 25 °C. There junction-to-ambient resistance is 40 °C/W max for periods up to 10 s and 75 °C/W max in steady state; junction to lead is 24 °C/W max. The 3.1 W rating (2.0 W at 70 °C) follows from the 10 s figure and a 150 °C junction limit. Normalized on-resistance at 150 °C is about 1.44 to 1.48 times its 25 °C value (Figure 4)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pulsed current and avalanche",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The -56 A pulsed current, -33 A avalanche current and 54 mJ avalanche energy at 0.1 mH are repetitive ratings bounded by the 150 °C junction limit, at frequencies and duty cycles low enough that every pulse starts from TJ = 25 °C. Figures 9 to 11 give single-pulse safe operating area, single-pulse power and transient thermal impedance on the 1 in² board."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Body diode",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The body diode carries up to -4 A continuously and drops -0.7 V typical, -1 V max, at -1 A. At -14 A and 500 A/µs it recovers in 14 ns with 25 nC typical."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Uses",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Listed uses are load switches and PWM circuits."
  }
 ],
